acme-integration: Hey, we're seeing intermittent JSON parse errors when consuming streaming responses from Redwood. Client gets a truncated JSON object sometimes. Example: 

acme-integration: Request id `req_Kz12...` - our parser fails with 'unexpected end of input'. We get status 200 and the stream ends abruptly. Anyone seen this?

maya (support): Thanks — can you paste a minimal repro (curl or client log) and the request-id? Also which proxy/network path is in front of you?

acme-integration: Here's a minimal curl log from our edge (we're behind an nginx-based proxy):

```
curl -v "https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-..." \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"model":"rwd-2b","stream":true,"input":"{\\"action\\":\\"create\\"}"}'
* HTTP/2 200
* chunked, then closed by server
* read: connection reset by peer
```

acme-integration: Partial response we received (cut mid-object):

```
{"id":"msg_abc","choices":[{"delta":{"content":"{\\"result\\":{\\"id\\":\\"123\\",\\"name\\":\\"acme\\"},\\"status\\":\\"ok\\"}"},"finish_reason":null}]}
{"id":"msg_abd","choices":[{"delta":{"content":"}\\n"},"finish_reason":"stop"}]
```

acme-integration: Sometimes the last chunk never arrives, client sees incomplete JSON (missing closing braces).

maya (support): Got it. @jonas / @lisa tagging eng — this smells like an intermediate proxy/buffer/window issue rather than Redwood runtime, but need logs to confirm. Can you also share nginx config or proxy timeouts?

jonas (eng-runtime): Hi — a few notes from runtime side: we stream using chunked transfer-encoding; we do not send Content-Length for streaming. If a proxy buffers until upstream closes or reaches a buffer limit and then tears the connection, that can result in incomplete bodies delivered to the client. Also if upstream hits an internal error and closes the connection without sending the final chunk, the client sees a truncated stream.

jonas (eng-runtime): Quick repro suggestion: set up a minimal nginx with default proxy_buffering on and a short `proxy_read_timeout` and try to reproduce. If nginx buffers and then upstream closes, client gets truncated chunk. AES: sample nginx snippet that caused us trouble in past:

```
location / {
  proxy_pass http://redwood-upstream;
  proxy_buffering on;
  proxy_buffers 4 16k;
  proxy_read_timeout 15s;
}
```

jonas (eng-runtime): Recommended quick mitigation: disable proxy_buffering for streaming endpoints and increase `proxy_read_timeout` and `keepalive_timeout`. Example:

```
proxy_buffering off;
proxy_read_timeout 120s;
proxy_send_timeout 120s;
```

lisa (devex): Also consider making the application tolerant to mid-stream failures: use NDJSON or event-delimited JSON where each event is a complete JSON object per line. That way a truncated final line won't break the whole parser. If switching is hard, add a finalization handshake in your client to detect incomplete payloads.

acme-integration: Thanks — we'll try disabling proxy_buffering and bumping timeouts. One more thing: any server-side logs we can fetch for `req_Kz12...`? The SDK UI only showed success.

deploy-bot: request log available: https://console.redwood.ai/requests/req_Kz12... (redacted)

maya (support): Thanks @acme-integration — I pulled the request trace. It shows the upstream returned several chunks and then connection was reset from your IP path. There are corresponding timeout spikes at the time window. I'll attach sanitized trace to the ticket.

maya (support): Next steps:
- acme to test nginx `proxy_buffering off` + increased timeouts and report results
- if still reproducible, we'll ask for tcpdump/http dump and escalate to eng-runtime with full trace
- eng to add a diagnostic flag to the runtime to log final chunk/connection close reason for a short window

jonas (eng-runtime): I'll add a short-lived trace mode to capture chunking and connection close reasons and coordinate with infra to correlate with your request-id. If we find a runtime bug (e.g., premature connection close on some error path) we'll notify here.

acme-integration: Will test in the next hour and report back. Appreciate the quick triage. :thumbsup:

lisa (devex): If you want a quick client-side guard, here's a tiny JS pattern to detect truncated JSON when streaming NDJSON-like payloads:

```
let buffer = '';
for await (const chunk of stream) {
  buffer += chunk;
  if (buffer.endsWith('\n')) {
    // parse safe line
    const lastLine = buffer.trim().split('\n').pop();
    try { JSON.parse(lastLine); } catch(e) { /* handle corrupted last line */ }
    buffer = '';
  }
}
```

maya (support): I'll keep this thread open. If you still hit failures after proxy changes, include a tcpdump or HAR export and we'll escalate to full postmortem.

acme-integration: Updated nginx to `proxy_buffering off` and `proxy_read_timeout 120s` — initial tests look good, no truncation in ~30 runs. We'll run a canary for 24h and report if we see regressions. Thanks all.
